      <tei:div corresp="urn:x-opensiddur:text:siddur:chol/minchah/tachanun">
        <tei:head xml:lang="en">Tachanun</tei:head>
        <tei:note type="instruction" xml:lang="en"><tei:hi rend="italic">Tachanun</tei:hi> is also omitted in the presence of a bridegroom, in the house of a mourner during the week of mourning, and on the occasion of a circumcision if the father, the <tei:hi rend="italic">sandak</tei:hi> or the <tei:hi rend="italic">mohel</tei:hi> is present. The worshipper recites the <tei:hi rend="italic">Tachanun</tei:hi> in a sitting position, while resting his brow on his arm. During the morning service, when the hand-<tei:hi rend="italic">tefillin</tei:hi> is on the left arm, he leans his brow on his right arm, but at the Mincha service he leans his brow on his left arm. This "falling on one's face" symbolizes complete submission to God and the committing of our destinies into His hands. It is mentioned in the Talmud (<tei:hi rend="italic">Maseches Megillah</tei:hi> 22b), and originates with Moses, who "Fell down before God" (Deuteronomy 9:18) and was followed by Joshua, who "Fell on the earth upon his face before the Ark of God" (Joshua 7:6)</tei:note>
